<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Diana (Mérida)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of Diana is a Roman temple at Mérida in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a>, capital of Lusitania. Built in the late 1st century BCE in the city's municipal forum, it was probably dedicated to the imperial cult, and its traditional name is a mistaken attribution. Its granite columns survived because a 16th-century palace was built inside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-diana-merida.jpg" alt="Temple of Diana (Mérida)" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Diana_in_M%C3%A9rida.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
